This rewrite is using a completely new infrastructure, with some elements to be copied from the reference implementations.

Overview:

We will adopt the collector system, there is an implementation in reference/old2, and also in reference/old3.

Every process started for the Test2::Harness2 namespace will be run through a collector.

A collector is a process that starts a child process with its stdour and stderr redirected to pipes that the collector reads from. Atomic::Pipe with zstd compression is used for sending atomic message (events) directly from the collected process to the collector over the stdout pipe with a sync message also sent on the stderr pipe (atomically) to help keep the pipes in sync. The collected process also sends regular output over the STDOUT and STDERR pipes which are muxed with the atomic messages. This is done via mized-message-mode on Atomic::Pipe

Read the Atomic::Pipe code in ~/projects/Atomic-Pipe to answer questions about how it works, how it does zstd compression, preserving zstd form of messages when needed.

Read the 2 old2 and old3 reference implementations for previosu Collector iterations.

New collector implementation will adopt the following:

We start a collector process, then code like this:

    my $pid = fork // die "Could not fork: $!"
    return if $pid; # Parent process returns

    # In collector process
    require Test2::Harness2::Collector;
    Test2::Harness2::Collector->start(
        exec => \@EXEC, # Option to start a collected process by fork+exec (or system(1, ...) on windows)
        run  => sub { ... }, # Option to instead start the collected process by running a subroutine
        parser => 'Test2::Harness2::Collector::Parser::...', # Required
        auditor => 'Test2::Harness2::Collector::Auditor::...', # Optional
        recorder => 'Test2::Harness2::Collector::Recorder::...', # Required
    );

    # Should not be able to reach this

The collector will create the pipes and fork:
    In the child: replace IO with the pipes, then run the exec or the subroutine
    In the collector: Start listening to the pipes
        Messages -> parser -(as events)-> [auditor] -> recorder
        Messages are read as or converted to events by the parser, events can then optionally be processed by the auditor, which may inject its own events, then the events go to the recorder for final handling.

The collector also waits on the collected process to collect its exit code. Once the child process has exited the collector will also exit, after all events ahve been handled. The collector exits 0 unless it encounters an internal error. Collectors should work very hard to catch any exceptions and report them (print to real STDERR, and insert an event into the event stream), but continue. They should intercept all signals, pass them along to the collected process but keep going waiting for the collected process to finish.

There are 3 types of process Test2::Harness2 may start, 'Services', 'Test Jobs', and 'Spawns', the first 2 are collected using collectors, the third is not and will be explained later. Services have no auditor, test jobs have an auditor.

The recorder object will accept the event stream and "record" it, possibly to a file, possibly to a database, possibly both. It also has a method that gets called to record the exit code, and the auditor can call a method on the recorder to record state transitions (to be defined later).

For initial development and testing we can add a t/script/collector script: collector Parser::Class Recorder::Class [Auditor::Class] -- EXEC COMMAND PARAMS
And we can have a temporary recorder class that writes everything to files (an events.jsonl file, a state.jsonl file, and an exit file)

The recorder will also need to watch for events that include files like screenshots and handle them.

We want to mostly copy the parsers and auditor from the reference/old3 implementations.

