Test2::Harness2 is a handle around the database and interface to starting services.

First iteration will use SQLite3, but we will keep things such that other databases can easily be used eventually.

For initial development we will asusme a new sqlite3 database will be created every time the harness is started.

Interface:

    my $h = Test2::Harness2->new();

start() begins by creating the db, this also serves as the file used for IPC discovery:

    ${SYSTEM_TMPDIR}/${USER}-${PROJECT}-${PID}.t2h2 - An SQLite database  (In the future this may not be created if given a DSN for another database)

It also adds a row to the 'runners' table.

Test2::Harness2 will not longer be a service/process on its own.

Alternatively instead of calling start one can use ->connect($dsn or $sqlite_file) to get a handle on an existing database/instance.

This handle can then be used to start/stop services, queue runs, cancel runs, query state, etc.

So for a proper usable instance one might:

    # Creates SQLite database (or connects to existing one if one is passed in)
    my $h = Test2::Harness2->new();

    # Inserts runner row, not finalized (still able to accept runs)
    my $r = $h->new_runner();

    # this will fail if the runner is already finalized
    # Scheduler is a service, only 1 scheduler can be present per runner, service name 'scheduler'
    my $s = $r->start_scheduler(
        resources => [
            [$resource_class, \%resource_settings],
            ...
        ],

        launchers => [
            [$launcher_class, $launcher_name, \%launcher_settings],
            ...
        ],
    );

    # Error if scheduler is finalized
    my $run = $s->queue_run(
        jobs => [
            {...},
            {...},
        ],

        resources => [
            [$resource_class, \%resource_settings],
            ...
        ],

        launchers => [
            [$launcher_class, $launcher_name, \%launcher_settings],
            ...
        ],

        env => {...}, # Env vars for all jobs
    );

    $r->finish(); # blocking, tells the scheduler no more runs are comming so it closes itself when done with runs in queue

    $run->refresh;
    return $run->result;

Tables(more columns will be needed, this schema is incomplete):

    users:
        - name
        - email

    hosts:
        - name      -- system hostname

    instances: Table for each instance, initially in our sqlite databases there will only ever be one entry, the one for the Test2::Harness2 instance..
        - instance_uuid -- uuid for the instance
        - host_id       -- link to the hosts table
        - user_id       -- link to the users table
        - started       -- timestamp
        - finished      -- timestamp
        - meta          -- JSON
        - finalized     -- timestamp, set if we know nothing else is going to be scheduled so things should wrap up and exit when they have nothing left to do, null before then.

    collectors: Row added when collector starts, exit code and stop time set by collector when the process it monitors exits
        - runner_id     -- links to runners table
        - name          -- Name of the collector (unique per runner)
        - pid           -- PID of the collector
        - watched       -- PID of the process being collected
        - type          -- (service, test job, etc)
        - start time    -- Time the collected process started
        - stop time     -- Time the collected process stopped
        - exit code     -- Exit code from the collected process once it terminates
        - finalized     -- timestamp, defined when we know the collector process is done, null before then

    artifacts: Files produced by collectors, like events.jsonl.zst, screenshots, etc.
        - collector_id  -- Collector artifact is assoicated with
        - filename      -- name of the file
        - content       -- content payload  (if not in a file on disk)
        - local_path    -- local path to file content (if not in 'content' field)
        -- Must set either 'content' or 'local_path' not both.

    services:
        - collector_id  -- link to collectors table
        - runner_id     -- link to runners table
        - run_id        -- link to run_id table, null for global services
        - name          -- unique per runner_id + run_id, example 'Scheduler'
        - class         -- perl class implementing the service

    service_state:  Each service adds a row upon state change, when querying state always find most recently inserted
        - service_id
        - stamp         -- timestamp
        - status        -- enum ('starting', 'up', 'down', 'stopping', 'stopped', 'broken')
        - content       -- json,  This will mainly be used for resource-services to convey a state that is used when assigning resources from the service.

    requests:
        - service_id
        - requested     -- timestamp of the request
        - completed     -- timestamp of the response (or when completed even if no response)
        - finalized     -- timestamp of the response (or when completed even if no response)
        - payload       -- json
        - response      -- json

    projects:
        - name

    versions:
        - version
        - project_id

    runs: Each 'run' that gets queued/executed gets a row
        - run_uuid      -- uuid for the run
        - runner_id     -- Link to Runner table
        - project_id    -- Link to the projects table
        - version_id    -- Link to the versions table
        - user_id       -- Link to the users table
        - run_ord       -- Runs are ordered, give them an ord integer. Unique per runner_id
        - started       -- timestamp, undef until the run has been started (used/set by scheduler)
        - finished      -- timestamp, undef until the run has completed (used/set by scheduler)
        - result        -- Bool, undef if not completed, true if all jobs passed, false if any jobs failed
        - passed        -- Int, number of jobs that passed
        - failed        -- Int, number of jobs that failed
        - meta          -- JSON
        - abort         -- bool, when set to true the scheduler should notice and stop requesting launches of further jobs and should terminate in-flight ones (via collector terminate flag)

    test_files:
        - relative      -- Relative path to test from project root
        - project_id    -- Link to projects table

    jobs: All info needed to launch the job
        - run_id        -- Link to Run table
        - test_file_id  -- Link to test_files table
        - spec          -- json

    job_tries: All state for a specific try at running the job
        - job_id
        - try_ord           -- Try number, starting at 1
        - started           -- timestamp, undef until the job has been started (used/set by scheduler)
        - finished          -- timestamp, undef until the job has completed (used/set by scheduler)
        - collector_id      -- link to the collectors table
        - result            -- Bool, undef if not completed, true if all jobs passed, false if any jobs failed
        - passed            -- Count of passed assertions
        - failed            -- Count of failed assertions
        - subtests          -- Count of top-level subtests
        - subtests_passed   -- Count of top-level subtests that passed
        - subtests_failed   -- Count of top-level subtests that failed

    launchers: Processes that are capable of launching test jobs (preloads, default launcher)
        - name              -- Launcher name
        - class             -- perl class implementing the launcher
        - spec              -- json, specification for launcher construction
        - runner_id         -- Link to the runners table, required
        - run_id            -- Link to the runs table, null for a global launcher, set for a run-associated launcher
        - collector_id      -- Link to the collector process
        - spawn_socket      -- Set to a path to a unix socket if the launcher supports 'spawn' functionality

    launches: Scheduler adds a row heret to schedule a job or spawn (see `yath spawn`)
        - launcher_id       -- Launcher that should handle the request
        - job_id            -- Job to launch
        - requested         -- Time the row was added
        - started           -- Time the launcher started the process

service_state.content and service_state.status are NOT managed by scheduler,
they are managed by each service, they are the info the service wishes to
convey, single-writer is service, many consumers. This is also how resource
services convey state to the resource classes in the scheduler.  General state
from Resource Service -> Resource object in scheduler via service_state.info
Other communication is resource object in scheduler process sending requests to
the resource service via the requests table

Requests can be deleted when the requestor reads the response (even when null,
so long as completed is set) unless there is a flag set to keep a record of
requets and responses, in which case 'finalized' is set instead of deleting the
row.

Scheduler updates run row for passed/failed/etc in realtime, and when run completes

