Concepts and roles:

Object::HashBase should be used for all objects, it is used to define attributes, constants for hash access.
Role::Tiny should be used for all roles.
Role::Tiny and Object::HashBase can be used together, Object::HashBase can be used inside roles, and can be used to consume roles that use Object::HashBase

Test2::Harness2::Collector                      - Starts a child process, reaps it, watches, parses and records stdout/stderr and events. For test jobs it also audits them
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Role::Parser        - Role parsers must implement
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Role::Auditor       - Role auditors must implement
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Role::Recorder      - Role recorders must implement
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Parser::IOParser    - Basic IO parser that turns STDERR and STDOUT into events, and also reads events sent as bursts over the Atomic::Pipe system
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Parser::TAPParser   - Subclass of IOParser that also parses TAP from STDOUT/STDERR
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Auditor::Test       - Auditor for test output
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Recorder::Files     - Simple example recorder mainly used to test the collector
Test2::Harness2::Collector::Recorder::DB        - Recorder used in actual runs that updates state in the database and writes events to an events.jsonl.zst that is listed in the db by filename, then when test is done the collector moves the content of the file to the db.

Test2::Harness2::Role::Service - Processes that have a main loop in which they
handle requests and get work done. The loop should iterate non-blocking as long
as there is work to do. When a loop completes with nothing to do it should
sleep to avoid busy work. To avoid hammering the database there should be a
backoff when there is nothing to do, so the sleep should be 0.05s, 0.1s, 0.5s,
then 1s being the longest between iterations. Services should also implement a
SIGUSR1 no-op handler so that SIGUSR1 can be sent by other processes to wake
them early when they are sleeping.

Test2::Harness2::Role::Launcher - Processes that start and wait for 'launches'
rows they need to act on, they set the 'started' stamp on the row when they
start it.Launchers will start and in a loop wait for something to launch
(launches table) and launch it. How they launch it is launcher specific. This
role also consumes the Test2::Harness2::Role::Service role.

Test2::Harness2::Launcher::ForkExec - Launch using fork+exec to start the collector, then the collector uses fork+exec to start the test process
Test2::Harness2::Launcher::Win32    - Launch using system(1, ...) window trick to start collector, collector uses same to start the test process
Test2::Harness2::Launcher::Default  - Default launcher, creates an instance of one of the 2 above (depending on environment) and delegates to it. Note that it delegates to the in-process object, it does not start a new process for the delegate launcher.
Test2::Harness2::Launcher::Preload  - The launcher process will run entirly in BEGIN with a Long::Jump to be able to return to the BEGIN, when a launch is requested it forks, uses Long::Jump to return to the begin then starts the collector which starts the child, the child then cleans up the process state and uses goto::file to swap in the test file code. See preloads in all reference/ implementations. it is CRITICAL that Long::Jump, goto::file and process cleanup happen, we cannot skip these or replace them with `do $file` or anything else. If implementation gets stuck and skipping one is considered STOP and discuss.

Launchers start test jobs with a collector for each test job, spawns (yath
spawn) do not get collectors. spawn's are disconnected (double fork, new
process group). Test process collectors DO NOT double-fork, launchers reap the
collector processes when they exit.

Collectors must update their row to populate the 'finalized' column before they
exit. This, like all db writes from the collector, must be done via the
recorder interface, allowing the 'Files' recorder to take alternate action for
testability without a database.

Preloads will do their loop in a BEGIN block and preload things, then use
goto::file and Long::Jump and state reset. See reference/ implementations.

Launcher classes (and/or role) must make it easier to start them, examples:
    perl -e 'use My::Launcher; My::Launcher->start'
    perl -e 'use Test2::Harness2::Launcher::Preload(); use Long::Jump(); BEGIN { my $out = Long::Jump::setjump(preload => sub { Test2::Harness2::Launcher::Preload->start }; ...; require goto::file; goto::file->import($out->{test_File}); Test2::Harness2::Launcher::Preload->reset_process_state; }'
or even easier, wrap it so you can do:
    open(my $fh, '|-', $^X, '-MTest2::Harness2::Launcher::Preload=start', '-e' => '1;') or die "$!"
    print $fh encode_json($preload_specification);
    close($fh);
or similar. Point is easy to start launchers as fresh processes.

Run state and scheduler state lives in the runs and job tables.

