The default collector-recorder will write events to a events.json.zst file,
each line is zst compressed, we can copy the zst implementation from
reference/old3. Also we can in many cases write through the caches zst form of
events that came over the Atomic::Pipe in compressed form. Exit code will be
recorded to the collector row in the database. Auditor will use state changes
to update the job_try row. Also an artifact entry will be created pointing at
the events.jsonl.zst file. When the collector is done it will use the recorder
to copy the content of the events.jsonl.zst file to the 'content' column of the
artifact table and remove the local_path column data.

Resource info is passed into start_scheduler() and queue_run(). Scheduler
manages resources, has the canonical instances, uses available() assing(), etc.

We will want to port over all the resource classes in reference/old3

Replacement temp dirs for tests (Setting the TMPDIR variable, etc) will become
a resource, it is always available, a directory is created and assigned to the
test, the directory is deleted upon release. No service is needed for the
resource.

A service process is simple, a loop that continually checks for requests in the
requests table that are for it, it then handles the requests and updated the
row with a response. requests that have no meaningful response may have the
response left null, but completed will be populated. Services also check their
'terminated' status on each loop and exit if it gets set. Services will also
implement a SIGUSR1 handler (on supported platforms, on unsupported platforms
this is skipped, it is an optimization, not a requirement). This signal is only
used to iterrupt sleeps when we know there is work to be done. The services
need to loop and check the database each time, we do not want to hammer the
database by pinging it ever loop with no sleeps, but we also do not want added
latency of sleeping when there is work to be done. On supported platforms other
processes that add rquests or set the termination flag can also send sigusr1 to
services to interrupt the sleep so it knows it has work to do and can query the
db. Services will usually have actions to take on every "tick".


Each table should get a simple object to represent it with the following methods:
 * $o->save       - Update any changed fields
 * $o->refresh    - Re-fetch row and update fields with changes

The Test2::Harness2 object should provide the following methods to get/create the objects:
 * $o = $h->fetch(table => %WHERE)
 * @os = $h->fetch_all(table => %WHERE)
 * @os = $h->insert(table => \%ROW1, \%ROW2, ...)

We do not want to depend on DBIx::Class, so this should be implemented with SQL
on DBI directly. Insert should be written to be very efficient.

Collectors and auditors can use SQL directly on their DBH handles, they do not
need to go through the Test2::Harness2 modules API. Optimize for performance.

There should be a launcher role that also consumes the service role. Collectors
spawned by the launcher should NOT reparent or double-fork, they should habdle
the collector exiting and send siguser1 (where supported) to the scheduler
service after updating the collector row to show is is sealed.

